---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ActionLabel from '../../components/ActionLabel.astro';
import '../../styles/guides-page.css';

const title = 'What’s moving in the market?';
const description = 'Read Market Tape with me. Check the dates, understand the scores, and find a useful next question for your market research or technology cost review.';
const articleUrl = 'https://cloudandcapital.com/insights/using-market-tape';
const articleData = {
  '@type': 'Article', '@id': `${articleUrl}#article`, headline: title, description,
  author: { '@id': 'https://cloudandcapital.com/#diana' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://cloudandcapital.com/#organization' },
  mainEntityOfPage: articleUrl, datePublished: '2026-10-23', dateModified: '2026-10-23',
  image: 'https://cloudandcapital.com/images/market-tape-equity-review.jpg',
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Cloud & Capital`} description={description} type="article" structuredData={[articleData]}>
  <main id="main-content" class="guides-page tape-guide">
    <article class="guide-shell guide-article">
      <header>
        <a class="guide-back" href="/insights">← Guides &amp; writing</a>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Markets &amp; technology finance · A tool walkthrough</p>
        <h1>What’s moving <em>in the market?</em></h1>
        <p class="guide-byline">By Diana Molski · <time datetime="2026-10-23">October 23, 2026</time></p>
        <p class="guide-lede">I built Market Tape to bring market moves and technology finance context into one place. Here’s how I’d use it to find something worth a closer look.</p>
        <p>It’s for people following the markets and people working on technology budgets. You don’t need to upload anything. Open the dashboard, check the dates, and choose the part that matters to you.</p>
        <a class="guide-button" href="https://market-tape.cloudandcapital.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-analytics-event="cta_click" data-analytics-destination="market_tape" data-analytics-source="market_tape_guide"><ActionLabel label="Open Market Tape" /></a>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="dates-title">
        <h2 id="dates-title">Start with the date.</h2>
        <p><strong>Prices dated</strong> tells you which trading date the daily price records belong to. <strong>Snapshot published</strong> tells you when the pipeline produced the file. Publishing a file later doesn’t make its prices newer.</p>
        <p>The pipeline is scheduled to check every 30 minutes during its weekday window. That’s a schedule, not a guarantee or a live quote feed. Daily prices can still change while the market is open. The infrastructure benchmarks and deal tracker have their own source dates.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="status-title">
        <h2 id="status-title">Get a feel for the snapshot.</h2>
        <p>Under <strong>Market Status</strong>, look at <strong>Equity ETF breadth</strong> and the share above the 20- and 50-session moving averages. They help you see how much of this tracked basket is participating.</p>
        <p>That reading uses 34 equity ETFs, counted once, and excludes SPY, bonds, commodities, yields, and volatility indices. On the October 22 snapshot, 16 of 34 had positive scores: 47/100, in the Middle band. Below 40 is the Lower band; 70 or above is the Upper band. These describe this sample, not how much money to invest.</p>
      </section>



      <figure class="guide-screenshot">
        <picture>
          <source media="(max-width: 760px)" srcset="/images/market-tape-snapshot-mobile.jpg" width="720" height="440" />
          <img src="/images/market-tape-equity-review.jpg" alt="Actual Market Tape equity ETF breadth: 16 of 34 positive scores, a reading of 47 out of 100 in the Middle band" width="1104" height="648" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption>A real dashboard capture from October 23, 2026, using the October 22 snapshot. Your visit may show different values.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section aria-labelledby="scores-title">
        <h2 id="scores-title">A score and a return are different things.</h2>
        <p><strong>RS1M</strong> compares an instrument’s price performance with SPY, an ETF that tracks the S&P 500, over 20 trading sessions, then adjusts for the instrument’s volatility. Positive means it outperformed SPY; negative means it underperformed. The size of the number is a score, not a percentage return.</p>
        <p>An instrument can fall in price and still have a positive score if SPY fell more. <strong>Macro Context</strong> keeps the actual 20-session percentage price change beside the score so you can tell them apart.</p>
        <p>The <strong>Sectors</strong>, <strong>Global ETF leaders</strong>, and <strong>Momentum</strong> lists rank these scores. An A, B, or C describes the price trend; it isn’t a business-quality grade or a buy/sell rating. The <a href="https://market-tape.cloudandcapital.com/sources" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">methodology page</a> explains the rules.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="example-title">
        <h2 id="example-title">Here’s a real example.</h2>
        <p>On the October 22, 2026 snapshot, consumer staples (XLP) led the sector score ranking. Meanwhile, the semiconductor ETFs SMH and SOXX had one-session price changes of about −6.0% and −6.8%. Those are daily price moves, separate from their 20-session relative-strength scores.</p>
        <p>I’d open <strong>All 48 tracked instruments</strong>, compare the 1D, 5D, and 20D columns, then check the funds’ holdings and recent news. Are the same companies driving both funds? Is the weakness limited to that day? The table helps me narrow the question; a single snapshot can’t establish a lasting rotation.</p>
      </section>

      <aside class="guide-example" aria-label="A next step for market research">
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">What would I do next?</p>
        <p>If defensive sectors rank above technology, I’d write down a question: <strong>Is this a broad shift, or are a few names driving it?</strong> Then I’d check the funds’ holdings and the underlying news. In the Momentum list, each ticker opens a Yahoo Finance research page. The ranking helps me choose what to research. It doesn’t answer the question by itself.</p>
      </aside>

      <section aria-labelledby="costs-title">
        <h2 id="costs-title">Working on a technology budget?</h2>
        <p>Look at the source-linked infrastructure benchmarks and AI compute tracker for wider context. Read the dates and the status of each announcement. Signed, announced, targeted, and reported deals are different things; the tracker is a selected, manually maintained list.</p>
        <p>Then use <strong>Before a cloud commitment</strong> to bring the discussion back to your own business. How much usage stays steady? What might change over three years? Can a job stop and restart? Those answers, along with the actual prices and contract terms, help you evaluate a commitment.</p>
        <p>Stock-market volatility can’t establish whether Spot capacity is safe for your workload. A software company’s valuation doesn’t tell you what discount its sales team will offer. Market context gives you questions to investigate alongside your own evidence.</p>
      </section>

      <figure class="guide-screenshot">
        <div class="phone-review-notes" aria-label="Questions from Before a cloud commitment">
          <div><h3>1-year commitment</h3><p>How much usage stays steady? Compare that baseline with the commitment, payment terms, and break-even point.</p></div>
          <div><h3>3-year commitment</h3><p>Will the workload still look like this in three years? Check migration plans, flexibility, and the cost of unused capacity.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Spot or on-demand</h3><p>Can the job stop and restart? Check interruption handling and fallback capacity. On-demand avoids a term commitment; it does not guarantee capacity.</p></div>
        </div>
        <img class="desktop-review-capture" src="/images/market-tape-cloud-review.jpg" alt="Market Tape’s fixed review questions for one-year commitments, three-year commitments, and Spot or on-demand capacity" width="1104" height="255" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Questions you can take into a cost review. These stay available when AI analysis is unavailable.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section aria-labelledby="lumen-title">
        <h2 id="lumen-title">Read Lumen with the numbers beside it.</h2>
        <p>Lumen adds an AI-written explanation of the snapshot. Check its numbers, units, dates, and conclusions against the dashboard and linked sources. It can still get things wrong.</p>
        <p><strong>Refresh analysis</strong> checks the analysis of the displayed snapshot. A message tells you if the same cached reading is still current; it doesn’t fetch a live market quote. If the underlying snapshot has changed, reload the page. If analysis is unavailable, you can still use the dated data and fixed review questions.</p>
        <p>If every current valuation is unavailable, that section stays hidden. Any older figure labeled Archived is historical context. New analysis pauses when a snapshot is more than four days old; that backstop doesn’t replace checking the dates yourself.</p>
      </section>

      <footer class="guide-next">
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Find your next question</p>
        <h2>Take a look, then follow the source.</h2>
        <p>Try a market question in Market Tape. If you’re reviewing a real technology bill or commitment, I can help you work through the costs, usage, and terms.</p>
        <div class="guide-actions">
          <a class="guide-button" href="https://market-tape.cloudandcapital.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><ActionLabel label="Explore the snapshot" /></a>
          <a href="/services#technology-cost-review"><ActionLabel label="Explore a cost review" /></a>
        </div>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .tape-guide .guide-button { background:#dce4d9; border-color:#a8b8a5; }
  .tape-guide .guide-screenshot img { background:var(--cream); border:1px solid #c9c2b7; padding:1rem; }
  .phone-review-notes { display: none; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .desktop-review-capture { display: none; }
    .phone-review-notes { display: grid; gap: 1.25rem; padding: 1.25rem; background: var(--cream); border: 1px solid #c9c2b7; }
    .phone-review-notes h3 { margin: 0 0 .5rem; font-size: 1.2rem; }
    .phone-review-notes p { margin: 0; font-size: .95rem; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .tape-guide .guide-screenshot picture { display: block; max-width: 23rem; margin-inline: auto; } .tape-guide .guide-screenshot picture img { width: 100%; height: auto; padding: .5rem; } }
  .tape-guide .guide-example { background:var(--cream); }
</style>
